"""
## Purpose and audience

This specification is maintained for compiler and library engineers reviewing UNIT-APP behavior, and for operators verifying a build.

## Operator workflow

Run `bin/simple test --no-session-daemon test/unit/app/protocol/jsonrpc_framing_spec.spl` and confirm the Results line reports 0 failed. Each scenario names the requirement it traces to and starts with a Verify step.

## Compatibility and limitations

Covers only the behavior explicitly asserted below on the current toolchain. No external standard or cross-version contract is claimed; numeric oracles are explained inline where they appear.

## Recovery and troubleshooting

If a scenario fails, re-run the single spec file, read the first failing Verify step, and compare the reported value against the inline oracle comment before bisecting recent changes.
"""
# @manual: primary
# REQ-UNIT-APP-001
# doc-path: doc/01_research/app/
# doc-path: doc/03_plan/app/
# doc-path: doc/04_architecture/app/
# doc-path: doc/05_design/app/
# @cover src/app/protocol/framing.spl 90%
# @req REQ-JSONRPC-FRAMING-001
"""
# JSON-RPC Content-Length Framing

**Category:** Infrastructure
**Status:** In Progress

## Purpose and Audience

Every Simple language server that speaks over stdio -- the MCP server, the LSP
bridges, the TRACE32 bridges, the DAP adapter -- wraps each JSON-RPC message in
an LSP-style `Content-Length:` header block. This specification fixes the
framing behaviour those servers share, so that an editor or agent host talking
to any of them sees the same wire behaviour.

The audience is anyone adding a new stdio server, or changing how an existing
one reads its input.

## Scope and Preconditions

This covers the pure framing primitives in `src/app/protocol/framing.spl`: the
header-line terminator strip, the decimal length parser, the Content-Length
header recogniser, and the outbound frame encoder. It does not cover the
per-server stdin read loops, which differ deliberately in their read primitive
and in whether they auto-detect bare JSON-lines input.

## Primary Workflow

A host writes `Content-Length: <n>`, a blank line, then `<n>` characters of JSON
body. The server reads header lines until the blank line, takes the declared
length from whichever line is the Content-Length header, and then reads exactly
that many characters. Replies are encoded the same way.

## Key Concepts

| Concept | Description |
|---------|-------------|
| Header block | Lines before the first blank line |
| Declared length | The number in the Content-Length header |
| Tolerant parse | A malformed header must never abort the server |

## Related Specifications

- `test/01_unit/app/mcp_unit/transport_tcp_spec.spl` -- the TCP transport variant

## Evidence and Provenance

Behaviour was read off the pre-merge implementations in `src/app/mcp/main.spl`
and `src/app/simple_lsp_mcp/json_helpers.spl`, which a byte-level diff showed to
be identical apart from function names. Divergent siblings are recorded in
`doc/08_tracking/bug/jsonrpc_framing_divergence_2026-08-11.md`.

## Recovery and Troubleshooting

A header the server cannot understand yields a zero or absent declared length;
the read loop then reports no message rather than reading a wrong number of
bytes off the stream. This is the deliberate failure mode: refuse the frame,
keep the stream position sane.

## Compatibility and Limitations

The declared length is counted in `text` characters, not UTF-8 bytes. For ASCII
JSON -- which is what these servers emit -- the two coincide. Non-ASCII bodies
are a known open gap and are not claimed here.
"""

use std.spec.*
use app.protocol.framing.*


describe "JSON-RPC Content-Length framing":

    it "reads the declared length from a well-formed header":
        # @req: REQ-UNIT-APP-001
            # @req: REQ-UNIT-APP-002
            # @req: REQ-UNIT-APP-003
        step("Verify: reads the declared length from a well-formed header")
        # @capture: assertion outcomes retained in the spec Results output as evidence
        step("a host sends a standard Content-Length header line")
        expect(frame_content_length_of("Content-Length: 42\r\n")).to_equal(42)  # oracle: expected value 42 is the behavior asserted by this regression spec

    it "accepts a header line that arrived without its terminator":
        # @req: REQ-UNIT-APP-004
        step("Verify: accepts a header line that arrived without its terminator")
        # @capture: assertion outcomes retained in the spec Results output as evidence
        step("the reader hands over a line whose CRLF was already consumed")
        expect(frame_content_length_of("Content-Length: 7")).to_equal(7)  # oracle: expected value 7 is the behavior asserted by this regression spec

    it "reports no length for a line that is not a Content-Length header":
        # @req: REQ-UNIT-APP-005
        step("Verify: reports no length for a line that is not a Content-Length header")
        # @capture: assertion outcomes retained in the spec Results output as evidence
        step("an unrelated header such as Content-Type arrives first")
        expect(frame_content_length_of("Content-Type: application/json\r\n")).to_equal(-1)  # oracle: expected value -1 is the behavior asserted by this regression spec

    it "tolerates an extra vendor header without mistaking it for a length":
        # @req: REQ-UNIT-APP-006
        step("Verify: tolerates an extra vendor header without mistaking it for a length")
        # @capture: assertion outcomes retained in the spec Results output as evidence
        step("a vendor extension header appears in the block")
        expect(frame_content_length_of("X-Simple-Trace: on\r\n")).to_equal(-1)  # oracle: expected value -1 is the behavior asserted by this regression spec

    it "treats a non-numeric length as zero rather than aborting":
        # @req: REQ-UNIT-APP-007
        step("Verify: treats a non-numeric length as zero rather than aborting")
        # @capture: assertion outcomes retained in the spec Results output as evidence
        step("a malformed header declares a word instead of a number")
        expect(frame_content_length_of("Content-Length: abc\r\n")).to_equal(0)  # oracle: expected value 0 is the behavior asserted by this regression spec

    it "stops at the first non-digit in a partly numeric length":
        # @req: REQ-UNIT-APP-008
        step("Verify: stops at the first non-digit in a partly numeric length")
        # @capture: assertion outcomes retained in the spec Results output as evidence
        step("a length is corrupted mid-value")
        expect(frame_content_length_of("Content-Length: 12x34\r\n")).to_equal(12)  # oracle: expected value 12 is the behavior asserted by this regression spec

    it "reads an empty length as zero":
        # @req: REQ-UNIT-APP-009
        step("Verify: reads an empty length as zero")
        # @capture: assertion outcomes retained in the spec Results output as evidence
        step("the header names no value at all")
        expect(frame_content_length_of("Content-Length:\r\n")).to_equal(0)  # oracle: expected value 0 is the behavior asserted by this regression spec

    it "accepts a length declared with no space after the colon":
        # @req: REQ-UNIT-APP-010
        step("Verify: accepts a length declared with no space after the colon")
        # @capture: assertion outcomes retained in the spec Results output as evidence
        step("a terse host omits the conventional space")
        expect(frame_content_length_of("Content-Length:15\r\n")).to_equal(15)  # oracle: expected value 15 is the behavior asserted by this regression spec

    it "removes a CRLF terminator without touching the rest of the line":
        # @req: REQ-UNIT-APP-011
        step("Verify: removes a CRLF terminator without touching the rest of the line")
        # @capture: assertion outcomes retained in the spec Results output as evidence
        step("a line ends in the protocol-standard CRLF")
        expect(frame_strip_line_end("Content-Length: 3\r\n")).to_equal("Content-Length: 3")

    it "removes a lone LF terminator":
        # @req: REQ-UNIT-APP-012
        step("Verify: removes a lone LF terminator")
        # @capture: assertion outcomes retained in the spec Results output as evidence
        step("a Unix-style reader delivers only a newline")
        expect(frame_strip_line_end("abc\n")).to_equal("abc")

    it "removes a lone CR terminator":
        # @req: REQ-UNIT-APP-013
        step("Verify: removes a lone CR terminator")
        # @capture: assertion outcomes retained in the spec Results output as evidence
        step("a split read delivers the CR without its LF")
        expect(frame_strip_line_end("abc\r")).to_equal("abc")

    it "leaves an unterminated line unchanged":
        # @req: REQ-UNIT-APP-014
        step("Verify: leaves an unterminated line unchanged")
        # @capture: assertion outcomes retained in the spec Results output as evidence
        step("the final line of a stream arrives with no terminator")
        expect(frame_strip_line_end("abc")).to_equal("abc")

    it "removes only one terminator so a split read is not over-trimmed":
        # @req: REQ-UNIT-APP-015
        step("Verify: removes only one terminator so a split read is not over-trimmed")
        # @capture: assertion outcomes retained in the spec Results output as evidence
        step("a buffer boundary leaves two terminators stuck together")
        expect(frame_strip_line_end("abc\n\n")).to_equal("abc\n")

    it "leaves an empty line empty":
        # @req: REQ-UNIT-APP-016
        step("Verify: leaves an empty line empty")
        # @capture: assertion outcomes retained in the spec Results output as evidence
        step("the blank line that closes the header block is stripped")
        expect(frame_strip_line_end("")).to_equal("")

    it "parses a bare decimal run":
        # @req: REQ-UNIT-APP-017
        step("Verify: parses a bare decimal run")
        # @capture: assertion outcomes retained in the spec Results output as evidence
        step("a length value is handed to the parser directly")
        expect(frame_parse_decimal("2048")).to_equal(2048)  # oracle: expected value 2048 is the behavior asserted by this regression spec

    it "reads a leading zero without changing the value":
        # @req: REQ-UNIT-APP-018
        step("Verify: reads a leading zero without changing the value")
        # @capture: assertion outcomes retained in the spec Results output as evidence
        step("a host zero-pads the declared length")
        expect(frame_parse_decimal("007")).to_equal(7)  # oracle: expected value 7 is the behavior asserted by this regression spec

    it "yields zero for text with no leading digit":
        # @req: REQ-UNIT-APP-019
        step("Verify: yields zero for text with no leading digit")
        # @capture: assertion outcomes retained in the spec Results output as evidence
        step("the length field is entirely non-numeric")
        expect(frame_parse_decimal("nope")).to_equal(0)  # oracle: expected value 0 is the behavior asserted by this regression spec

    it "yields zero for an empty length field":
        # @req: REQ-UNIT-APP-020
        step("Verify: yields zero for an empty length field")
        # @capture: assertion outcomes retained in the spec Results output as evidence
        step("the length field is empty")
        expect(frame_parse_decimal("")).to_equal(0)  # oracle: expected value 0 is the behavior asserted by this regression spec

    it "encodes a reply with a header, a blank line, and the body":
        # @req: REQ-UNIT-APP-021
        step("Verify: encodes a reply with a header, a blank line, and the body")
        # @capture: assertion outcomes retained in the spec Results output as evidence
        step("the server frames a JSON-RPC reply for the host")
        expect(frame_encode("{}")).to_equal("Content-Length: 2\r\n\r\n{}")

    it "declares a length that matches the body it encodes":
        # @req: REQ-UNIT-APP-022
        step("Verify: declares a length that matches the body it encodes")
        # @capture: assertion outcomes retained in the spec Results output as evidence
        step("the server frames a larger reply")
        val body = "{\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"id\":1}"
        expect(frame_encode(body)).to_equal("Content-Length: " + str(body.len()) + "\r\n\r\n" + body)

    it "encodes an empty body as a zero-length frame":
        # @req: REQ-UNIT-APP-023
        step("Verify: encodes an empty body as a zero-length frame")
        # @capture: assertion outcomes retained in the spec Results output as evidence
        step("the server has nothing to say but must still frame it")
        expect(frame_encode("")).to_equal("Content-Length: 0\r\n\r\n")

    it "round-trips its own encoded header back to the declared length":
        # @req: REQ-UNIT-APP-024
        step("Verify: round-trips its own encoded header back to the declared length")
        # @capture: assertion outcomes retained in the spec Results output as evidence
        step("a frame the server emitted is parsed by the same primitives")
        val body = "{\"ok\":true}"
        val header = frame_encode(body).split("\r\n")[0]
        expect(frame_content_length_of(header)).to_equal(body.len())
